# =============================================================================
# File: .env.config.example
# Version: 8
# Path: infra/k8s/overlays/dev/.env.config.example
#       (copy → .env.config, adjust values)
# Description: TEMPLATE for the dev overlay's NON-SECRET config source. The
#              kustomize configMapGenerator turns this into the `aywizz-config`
#              ConfigMap, injected into every pod via `envFrom`. Copy to
#              `.env.config` (git-ignored) and adjust. Symmetric to
#              `.env.secret.example` — SECRETS live there, NEVER here
#              (CLAUDE.md §4.6 / R-100-118 v2).
#
#              This file is the AUTHORITATIVE, hand-maintained schema of the
#              non-secret keys the platform reads. Every settings class uses
#              `extra="ignore"`, so unknown keys are harmless and most fields
#              have safe in-cluster defaults; the entries below are the ones
#              worth pinning explicitly for a working dev bring-up. Keys whose
#              in-cluster default is already correct are shown commented with
#              their default so the schema stays discoverable.
#
#              Two flags are injected as overlay LITERALS (see
#              kustomization.yaml), NOT here: C2_DEMO_SEED_ENABLED=true and
#              C2_UX_DEV_MODE_ENABLED=true. COMPONENT_MODULE is set per
#              Deployment (the one-image / N-containers discriminator), never
#              in this file.
# =============================================================================

# --- Platform -----------------------------------------------------------------
PLATFORM_ENVIRONMENT=development

# --- ArangoDB (C11) — shared unified store ------------------------------------
# In-cluster service DNS; the password is a SECRET (see .env.secret).
ARANGO_URL=http://arangodb:8529
ARANGO_DB=platform
ARANGO_USERNAME=ay_app

# --- MinIO (C10) — object / artifact store ------------------------------------
# The secret key is a SECRET (see .env.secret).
MINIO_ENDPOINT=minio:9000
MINIO_ACCESS_KEY=ay_app
MINIO_SECURE=false
# Per-component buckets have code defaults (orchestrator/requirements/validation/
# memory/llm-archive); the shared artifact bucket used by C8-admin storage
# metering is `orchestrator`.
MINIO_BUCKET=orchestrator

# --- Corporate HTTP proxy — MANDATORY where one is injected ------------------
# On a cluster that injects HTTP_PROXY / http_proxy into every pod (common on
# managed Kubernetes behind an enterprise egress), IN-CLUSTER traffic must be
# excluded or the proxy will try to route service names it cannot resolve and
# will CLOSE the connection. The symptom is brutal and misleading: MinIO's
# `mc` and the ollama CLI — both written in Go, which honours these variables
# natively — fail with "Connection closed by foreign host" while the servers
# are perfectly healthy (2026-09-17).
#
# WHY IT SPLITS CLIENTS APART, which is what made it so hard to see: a client
# that honours the proxy fails, one that ignores it succeeds, against the SAME
# endpoint. `mc`, the ollama CLI and Python's `urllib` failed; the MinIO
# Python SDK (urllib3 does not read these variables without an explicit
# ProxyManager), a raw socket, and `arangosh` all succeeded. That is why
# arangodb-init worked while minio-init did not.
#
# THE TRAP — `no_proxy` MATCHES BY SUFFIX AND NEVER COVERS A SINGLE-LABEL
# NAME. `.svc.cluster.local` does NOT match `minio`, and every URL in this
# file uses the short form. The bare service names must therefore be listed
# one by one. Regenerate the list after adding a Service:
#   kubectl kustomize infra/k8s/overlays/dev | grep -A1 'kind: Service'
#
# APPEND, NEVER REPLACE. A cluster that injects HTTP_PROXY also injects a
# NO_PROXY carrying entries that must survive — the cloud metadata endpoint
# (169.254.169.254), internal ranges, the API server. Setting this key here
# OVERWRITES the injected value for every pod reading this ConfigMap, so the
# value below must be the EXISTING one with the platform names appended:
#
#   1. read the current value:  env | grep -i no_proxy   (in any running pod)
#   2. append the platform list to it
#   3. paste the WHOLE result here, in both spellings
#
# That the injected list already covers part of the platform is not
# hypothetical: on 2026-09-17 in-cluster calls to `arangodb` went through
# while calls to `minio` and `ollama` did not, from the same pod and the same
# client — the difference being which names the injected NO_PROXY happened to
# list.
#
# WHAT NO LONGER DEPENDS ON THIS KEY. Every image this repo BUILDS appends
# its own in-cluster exclusions at process start, to whatever it inherits:
#   - the `_init` Jobs (minio-init, ollama-seed) — since minio-init v7 ;
#   - the whole api tier, via the prelude in infra/docker/Dockerfile.api v5
#     (c2..c9, c16, c8-admin, c8-cost-receiver, mock-llm) ;
#   - c13, via infra/c13_extractor/docker/Dockerfile v3.
# Those bootstrap correctly even with this key unset.
#
# WHAT STILL DEPENDS ON IT — the third-party images, whose entrypoint this
# repo does not own and must not replace: `litellm`, `c12` (n8n), `traefik`
# (c1-gateway) and the UI. Of those, litellm and n8n are the ones that
# genuinely make in-cluster calls (litellm → c8-cost-receiver and c8-admin ;
# n8n → c13-extractor and c7-memory), so leaving this unset degrades cost
# metering, credential resolution and ingestion — not the platform's boot.
#
# Note for litellm specifically: it must reach EXTERNAL providers THROUGH the
# proxy while bypassing it for in-cluster names. That is exactly what
# appending (rather than replacing) preserves, and exactly what setting a
# narrow NO_PROXY on that container alone would break.
#
# Set BOTH spellings: Go reads HTTP_PROXY/NO_PROXY, some tools only the
# lowercase form.
#
# Platform names to APPEND (suffixes first, then every Service — suffix
# matching never covers a single-label name, so `.svc` does not match `minio`):
# NO_PROXY=<existing value>,localhost,127.0.0.1,::1,.svc,.svc.cluster.local,.cluster.local,arangodb,ay-platform-ui,c1-gateway,c1-gateway-dashboard,c12,c12-workflow,c13-extractor,c16-backup,c2,c2-auth,c3-conversation,c4-orchestrator,c5-requirements,c6-validation,c7,c7-memory,c8-admin,c8-cost-receiver,c9-mcp,litellm,minio,ollama
# no_proxy=<the same full value>

# --- LLM egress (C8) — every component calls the gateway, NEVER a provider ----
# CRITICAL override: the client default is http://c8:8000/v1, but the real
# in-cluster proxy Service is `litellm:4000`. Point clients at it.
C8_GATEWAY_URL=http://litellm:4000/v1
# C8_GATEWAY_API_KEY is a SECRET (shared client credential + litellm master_key)
# — set it in .env.secret. In dev it may be any non-empty placeholder.
#
# MODEL SELECTION IS AUTOMATIC + IN-APP (D-011). The C8 client resolves each
# agent → a CONCRETE model from the operator's registry CATALOGUE, scoped to
# the call's PROJECT, by quality tier. So there is NO model/provider name in
# config: just register a provider + models in the HMI and associate them with
# the project — the platform picks the right one per call. The two knobs below
# are OPTIONAL legacy overrides (leave them UNSET for the in-app behaviour):
# C8_DEFAULT_MODEL=            # force a single model ALIAS for every agent
# C8_AGENT_ROUTES_INLINE=      # JSON {agent: alias} to pin per-agent routing

# --- C3 chat RAG wiring -------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED for automatic LLM answers. C3 does chat-with-RAG only when BOTH this
# C7 base URL AND C8_GATEWAY_URL (above) are non-empty; otherwise it saves the
# message and replies with the "RAG / LLM not wired" stub. Point it at the C7
# memory Service (name `c7-memory`, port 8000).
C3_C7_BASE_URL=http://c7-memory:8000
# OPTIONAL — enables the chat-direct DocGen tool loop (D-015): the LLM is
# offered the document tools, executed against C4. Empty → plain RAG chat.
C3_C4_BASE_URL=http://c4-orchestrator:8000

# --- Embeddings (C7) — IN-APP (D-011) -----------------------------------------
# Embedding selection is NOT env-configured. C7's bootstrap fallback is a fixed
# deterministic hash (lexical, no network). Real embedders (the dev-deployed
# Ollama + all-minilm, or an openai-compatible endpoint) are declared in the
# embedding registry via the admin HMI and selected per-project. The dev stack
# still deploys Ollama + the ollama-seed Job so that model is available to
# register.
#
# The ONE env knob in this area. It is the base URL the dev SEED writes onto
# the pre-registered Ollama embedding provider, so it is read exactly once, at
# C8-admin startup, and only while the seed is enabled — `C8_SEED_OLLAMA_EMBEDDING`,
# set as a literal in overlays/dev/kustomization.yaml, not here.
#
# IT IS NOT A GLOBAL REDIRECT. Changing it does not move an ALREADY-SEEDED
# provider: the seed is idempotent on absence, and the stored `base_url` is
# operator-editable in the HMI afterwards. To repoint an existing provider,
# edit it there.
#
# KEEP THE SCHEME AND THE PORT. Ollama serves plain HTTP on 11434. An `https://`
# value resolves to port 443, where nothing listens, and the symptom surfaces
# several layers away as `context deadline exceeded` on a health check rather
# than as an error at the point of configuration (seen on AKS 2026-09-17,
# from a hand-entered `https://ollama`).
# C8_OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434   # default; uncomment only to override

# --- C2 Auth ------------------------------------------------------------------
# SEMANTIC (§4.6). `local` = username/password against C2's own store (dev
# default); `none` = trust forward-auth headers as-is; `sso` = Entra ID.
C2_AUTH_MODE=local
C2_JWT_ALGORITHM=HS256
# C2_JWT_SECRET_KEY, C2_LOCAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD, C2_LOCAL_PLATFORM_MANAGER_PASSWORD
# are SECRETS — see .env.secret. Usernames are non-secret. The platform_manager
# is bootstrapped only when BOTH its username and password are non-empty:
C2_LOCAL_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
C2_LOCAL_PLATFORM_MANAGER_USERNAME=platform-manager

# --- C4 Orchestrator ----------------------------------------------------------
# SEMANTIC (§4.6). `in-process` runs sub-agents inside the orchestrator pod;
# `k8s` fans them out to ephemeral SANDBOX pods and REQUIRES the opt-in
# c4_workers layer — install it with `infra/k8s/run.sh dev --workers`.
# The orchestrator gets Pod lifecycle in the `c4-workers` namespace through a
# NAMESPACED Role; no cluster-scoped permission is involved.
C4_DISPATCHER_BACKEND=k8s
# The C4_K8S_* defaults in c4_orchestrator/config.py describe the COMPOSE
# view (`ay-api:local`, `host.docker.internal`) and do NOT work in-cluster.
#
# The endpoints below MUST be fully qualified, unlike every other Service
# reference in this file: sandbox pods live in `c4-workers`, and a bare
# Service name only resolves within the caller's OWN namespace. With the
# short form the pod dies on `Failed to resolve 'minio'` before reaching its
# runtime.
#
# ...and a fully qualified name necessarily carries a namespace, which would
# pin this file to ONE installation. `{namespace}` is therefore substituted
# by C4 at startup with the namespace the ORCHESTRATOR itself runs in — read
# from the service-account projection every pod carries, so it is correct
# wherever the platform is installed and needs no RBAC. MinIO and LiteLLM run
# beside the orchestrator, which is what makes its own namespace the right
# one. Write a literal namespace instead and it is used verbatim.
#
# NOT the same namespace as `C4_K8S_NAMESPACE` below, which is where the
# SANDBOX pods are created. The two coincide today only because the cluster
# grants a single namespace.
C4_K8S_IMAGE=ghcr.io/ayfondation/aywizz-api:latest
# SEMANTIC (§4.6). Namespace hosting the sandbox pods. `aywizz` = the
# platform namespace itself, required when the cluster grants only ONE
# namespace. Trade-off recorded in base/c4_workers/rbac.yaml: `create pods`
# in a namespace is equivalent to READ on every Secret of that namespace,
# so this hands the orchestrator an indirect path to `aywizz-secrets`.
# Point it at a dedicated namespace as soon as the environment allows one.
C4_K8S_NAMESPACE=aywizz
C4_K8S_POD_VIEW_MINIO_ENDPOINT=minio.{namespace}.svc.cluster.local:9000
C4_K8S_POD_VIEW_C8_GATEWAY_URL=http://litellm.{namespace}.svc.cluster.local:4000/v1
# SEMANTIC (§4.6). `in_process` (default) vs `openhands` V2 generate engine.
C4_GENERATE_ENGINE=in_process
# NATS is only needed by the K8s dispatcher / event bus; harmless otherwise.
# C4_NATS_URL=nats://nats:4222

# --- UI / gateway public origin ----------------------------------------------
# Base URL the UI + C12 webhooks advertise. Matches the dev LoadBalancer.
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:56000

# --- Optional integrations (leave unset unless used) --------------------------
# Gitea (DocGen live-docs versioning). GITEA_ROOT_PASSWORD would be a SECRET.
# C2_GITEA_BASE_URL=http://gitea:3000
# C4_GITEA_BASE_URL=http://gitea:3000
# GITEA_ROOT_USERNAME=gitea_admin
